            <ArticleTitle>Interface and material engineering for zigzag slab lasers.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>16699</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s41598-017-16968-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Laser damage of zigzag slab lasers occurs at interface between laser crystal and SiO2 film. Although an additional HfO2 layer could be used to manipulate electric-field on the crystal-film interface, their high absorption and polycrystalline structure were unacceptable. SiO2 was then doped in HfO2 to suppress its crystallization and to achieve low absorption by annealing. HfxSi1-xO2 nanocomposite layers were then inserted between laser crystal and SiO2 film to minimize electric-field at crystal-film interface. Laser damage resistance of this new architecture is two times higher than that of traditional zigzag slab lasers.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
